   including the call and return.

   Optimizations applied:
   - For each k, we take a *snapshot* of row k and column k into
     contiguous temporary buffers row_k[] and col_k[].  For the
     PolyBench initialization (all weights >= 1 or 999), diagonal
     entries remain non-negative, so these snapshots are equivalent
     to using path[i][k] and path[k][j] directly in the original
     in-place formulation of Floyd–Warshall.
   - We then update the (i,j) space using these snapshots:
         path[i][j] = min(path[i][j], col_k[i] + row_k[j]);
     This removes intra-k dependencies among (i,j) updates and
     greatly improves cache behavior.
   - The (i,j) iteration space is blocked (tiled) to increase data
     locality for path[][] and to give the compiler a simple,
     unit-stride inner loop over j that is easy to vectorize.
   - We hoist col_k[i] out of the inner j-loop so it can stay in
     a register, and use a branchless min (ternary operator) in
     the innermost loop, which maps well to SIMD min/select.
