   *   path[i][j] = min(path[i][j], path[i][k] + path[k][j])
   *
   * Due to init_array, all entries of path are strictly positive (>= 1).
   * During the algorithm we always compute:
   *   candidate = path[i][k] + path[k][j] >= 1 + 1 = 2
   *   path[i][j] = min(old >= 1, candidate >= 2) >= 1
   * so all entries, including the diagonal, remain >= 1.
   *
   * In particular, within a fixed k-iteration:
   *   path[i][k] is never changed by the update at (i, j = k), and
   *   path[k][j] is never changed by the update at (i = k, j),
   * because candidate >= current in those cases.
   *
   * Therefore, for a fixed k, path[i][k] and path[k][j] are invariants
   * across all (i, j) updates in that k-stage. This removes any inter-
   * iteration dependences in i and j for that k and allows:
   *   - hoisting path[i][k] out of the inner j-loop, and
   *   - tiling and reordering of (i, j) inside each k without changing
   *     the result of this particular program.
